<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gorm the Old</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 958</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gorm the Old was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from about 936 until his death around 958, and is regarded as the founder of the continuous Danish royal line. He ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>, where he raised a runestone in memory of his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thyra">Thyra</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a> succeeded him, enlarged the royal monuments at Jelling and made Denmark Christian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gorm-the-old.jpg" alt="Gorm the Old" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: August Carl Vilhelm Thomsen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:August_Thomsen_-_Thyra_Dannebod_meddeler_Kong_Gorm_den_Gamle_Underretning_om_hans_S%C3%B8n_Knuds_D%C3%B8d.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
